  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The $6$th root of unity ${e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}={z}^{p},\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal $6$th root of unity. What is the value of $p?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $6$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/6}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,\u2026,5.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* $6$th root of unity by substituting $k=1:$\n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/6}={e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3} & =({e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3})^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4.$",
